NAME
       kill - send a signal to processes

SYNOPSIS
       kill [-s SIGNAL | -SIGNAL | -n NUMBER] PID|%JOB...
       kill -l [SIGNAL]

DESCRIPTION
       kill sends a signal to each process given by its process ID (PID), or
       to a job of this shell (%1). Despite its name it can send any signal,
       but the default, SIGTERM (15), asks the process to end. A well-written
       program cleans up and exits.

       Bash has kill built in; it works the same as /usr/bin/kill and also
       understands %JOB. Normal users may signal only their own processes;
       root may signal any.

       Find PIDs with ps, pgrep or top. To signal processes by name, use
       pkill or killall.

   Signals worth knowing
       1  HUP     hang up; many services reload their configuration on it
       2  INT     interrupt, what Ctrl+C sends
       9  KILL    end at once; cannot be caught or ignored. Use it only when
                  TERM does not work: the program gets no chance to clean up
       15 TERM    please end (the default)
       18 CONT    continue a stopped process
       19 STOP    stop (pause) a process; cannot be ignored
       20 TSTP    the stop that Ctrl+Z sends

       A signal can be written as a number (-9), a name (-KILL) or a name with
       SIG (-SIGKILL).

OPTIONS
       -s SIGNAL
              The signal to send, by name or number.

       -SIGNAL
              The same, written short: -9, -KILL, -HUP.

       -n NUMBER
              The signal by number.

       -l [SIGNAL]
              List the signal names (or translate one name or number).

       -L     List the signals as a table.

EXAMPLES
       Ask process 1234 to end:

           kill 1234

       If it does not, end it by force:

           kill -9 1234

       Make a service's main process reread its configuration:

           kill -HUP $(pgrep rsyslogd)

       Stop and continue background job 1:

           kill -STOP %1
           kill -CONT %1

       End the process that uses the most CPU (look first, then kill):

           ps -eo pid,%cpu,comm --sort=-%cpu | head -5
           kill PID

SEE ALSO
       killall(1), pkill(1), ps(1), top(1), signal(7)
